{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261025T173802Z-7a0e5c49", "ts": "2026-10-25T17:45:22Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.7.3-dev.1", "branch": "feature/1052-evidence-transport"}, "body": {"base_commit": "8fbdfbc6d0d6cb3ad177008b020c54b4856db98b", "base_tree": "0f9260e6c405ad8533e59b81f89ac055496a70a8", "head_tree": "d8f59221836a803228f17987d8e08f549b8c42df", "head_commit": "aba0d1ec1c669a5dbdad7a00140001fa57392fb4", "dispatch_commit": "aba0d1ec1c669a5dbdad7a00140001fa57392fb4", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args final redirected: working tree is clean, so chunk/final would dispatch an empty interval; branch is 8 commit(s) ahead of origin/develop and no cumulative record covers current HEAD \u2014 the committed bundle is cumulative's scope", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py", "tests/test_plan_lifecycle.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Test evidence is stale for the reviewed tree", "recommendation": "`prawduct-hook test-status` exits 1: `stale: evidence predates session (2026-10-25T15:18:31Z < 2026-10-25T16:25:06Z)`. The integration commit aba0d1ec (evidence.review_anchor moved, six call sites rewired across plan_status, plan_lifecycle, evidence_notes, critic_consolidate x2, coverage.count_branch_rounds) has no recorded suite run. Run the suite through `testament run` and record it before this bundle merges. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/coverage.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Skew-guard refusal of `evidence ingest` is pinned only at the predicate, not at the wiring", "recommendation": "The skew guard now refuses `evidence ingest` only because `_dispatch` threads `sys.argv[2:]` into `_check_binary_skew` and that calls `_is_data_plane_write`. The only test is `TestIngestIsADataPlaneWrite.test_ingest_is_a_data_plane_op_and_status_is_not`, and it calls the predicate directly. If the call site reverted to `_check_binary_skew(command, project_dir)`, `argv or []` would make `evidence ingest` a non-write and it would append on a foreign binary, while the suite stayed green. tests.md says 'Fix and pin a defect at the LAYER IT WAS REPORTED AT \u2014 a test on the extracted predicate proves the predicate, not the wiring'. The ephemeral guard got an end-to-end test (`test_ingest_is_refused_inside_a_disposable_agent_worktree`) but the skew guard did not. Add a case to `tests/test_binary_skew_guard.py::TestFrameworkCheckoutSkew` that runs `evidence ingest` on the foreign binary and expects `_SKEW_MARKER` and exit 1, with `evidence status` beside it as the control that proceeds. Scope: instance.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "tests/test_evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "A fact whose note was not written can never be re-sent: the retry paths skip attach", "recommendation": "In `critic_consolidate.consolidate`, the review note is attached only in the `if not already:` branch, and a resolution's note only after a fresh append (`has_fact` -> `continue`). A consolidation that appends and then dies, or whose attach errors (no committer identity, a blocked ref; the accepted NOTE case), leaves the fact in this clone's store with no note. A re-run cannot repair it, because the retry finds the fact already present and skips the attach. Nothing in Chunks 01-03 re-attaches either: `evidence sync` only fetches, merges, ingests and pushes. In every other clone, CI included, the interval stays uncovered until someone pays for a fresh review round. The plan's Goals promise a second clone reaches the same verdict from the same facts. The 'Known and accepted' entry accepts the NOTE, not this irrecoverability. Fix: on the already-appended paths, re-attach when the anchor's note lacks the line. Duplicate note lines are harmless: ingest dedupes by (kind, id), and Chunk 02's `cat_sort_uniq` merge drops them. Alternatively, give sync a step that re-attaches transported facts whose anchor note lacks them. Pin it with a retry-after-failed-attach test. Scope: class (every append-then-attach site: consolidate's review and resolution writes, and `plan import-status --apply`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Change fragment has an empty narrative (record-lint change-fragment)", "recommendation": "Relayed from the manifest's record_lint: `.prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md` \u2014 `empty-narrative: say what changed and why above the consumer note`. Write the narrative: what Chunks 01 and 04 changed (notes attached at consolidation and import, `evidence ingest`, and `plan_lifecycle.observe`) and why. The plan routes the fragment's prose to Chunk 05, but the fragment fails its schema now, so it cannot ride a merge as it stands. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "Change fragment has an empty consumer note (record-lint change-fragment)", "recommendation": "Relayed from the manifest's record_lint: `.prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md` \u2014 `empty-consumer-note: write the note, or `none: <reason>` when there is nothing to tell consumers`. Consumers do have something to learn: a new `prawduct-hook evidence ingest` subcommand that is refused in disposable worktrees and on a foreign binary, and consolidation now writing `refs/notes/prawduct`. Write the note. Scope: instance.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "plan_lifecycle keeps a dead `plan_status` import that is now the only reason plan_status describes an import cycle", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the integration commit moved `review_anchor` from `plan_status` to `evidence`, and nothing removed the import it had needed. `plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py:74` still imports `plan_status`, but the module never references it (grep `plan_status\\.` there finds nothing; at 92afa6ef its one use was `plan_status.review_anchor`, line 412). Meanwhile `plugin/lib/plan_status.py:70-71` keeps a `TYPE_CHECKING` import of `plan_lifecycle`, commented 'plan_lifecycle imports this module'. That is true only because of the dead import. Consequence: Chunk 03 (B) wires `plan status` to `observe` and `render_lifecycle`. While the dead import stays, a runtime import of `plan_lifecycle` from `plan_status` is a real `plan_status -> plan_lifecycle -> plan_status` cycle, and the comment tells B to route around it. Remedy: drop `plan_status` from plan_lifecycle's import list, then either import `plan_lifecycle` normally in plan_status or keep the TYPE_CHECKING guard without the false cycle reason. `ruff`'s F401 is selected in pyproject.toml but does not gate yet, which is why this got through.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Which `evidence` ops write is stated twice, with opposite polarity, and one site bypasses the ops table built for it", "recommendation": "Class: the per-op write classification for one command. The skew guard reads a write-allowlist (`_DATA_PLANE_OPS = {\"evidence\": {\"ingest\"}}`). The ephemeral guard has a hand-written branch, `if command == \"evidence\": return op not in (None, \"status\", \"list\")`, that duplicates what `_EPHEMERAL_READ_ONLY_OPS` already does for `advisory`/`plan`. (A frozenset holding `None` covers the bare form.) Chunk 03 registers `evidence sync` and `evidence configure`. The ephemeral side then refuses them by default, but the skew side admits them unless someone remembers `_DATA_PLANE_OPS`. Also, `_ephemeral_command_writes`'s docstring question is 'would mutate state that dies with this worktree', and `ingest` writes the clone-shared store, which cannot strand. The refusal is a recorded decision (plan Decisions, Chunk 01), but the predicate no longer says what it decides. Remedy: one per-op table for `evidence`, its read set, from which both guards derive (data-plane write = not a read). Recording the refusal's real reason at the branch is a correction, not a deletion, so it belongs in this change rather than in Chunk 03.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-7"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "evidence CLI header still presents `evidence*` as a read-only allowlistable surface after a write op joined it", "recommendation": "Instance (prose ceiling). The comment at `plugin/lib/evidence.py:1446-1447` reads 'CLI (`prawduct-hook evidence <status|list>`) \u2014 the stable allowlistable surface (R4); skills scope to `Bash(prawduct-hook evidence*)`'. The `_CLI_USAGE` directly below it now includes `ingest`, a data-plane write. So the premise is false: the Critic skill's `Bash(prawduct-hook evidence*)` / `Bash(python3 plugin/bin/prawduct-hook evidence*)` grant now admits a store write from the reviewer, whose contract is never to mutate what it reviews. The plan carries narrowing that grant to Chunk 05. Fix the comment now (name `ingest` and say the glob is no longer read-only), because it is a correction, not a deferred deletion. Narrowing the grant to `status`/`list` closes the class.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "fid": "R-8"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "note", "title": "Two module docstrings describe wiring or reach that this commit does not yet have", "recommendation": "Instance (prose ceiling, nothing reads it). (1) `plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py:44-45`: '`plan status` prints it and `plan check-claims` reads its `merged-unreleased`'. At aba0d1ec nothing in plugin/ calls `observe`, and check-claims still derives release-pending through `_release_pending_records`. This becomes true when Chunk 03 lands, so no edit is wanted if 03 merges before the PR. If 03 slips, make it relational ('consumers read \u2026'). (2) `plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py:3-4`: 'lives inside `.git` and is never pushed, so every clone but the one that ran a review sees no coverage' is the pre-transport problem told in present tense. It will also match Chunk 05's acceptance grep for `never pushed` across plugin/. Chunk 05 should know this file is in that grep's set.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_lifecycle.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py"], "fid": "R-9"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "note", "title": "Integration residue: blank-line runs where `review_anchor` was removed, and `attach`'s `anchor: str` annotation versus its `None` callers", "recommendation": "Instance, cosmetic. Removing the two delegate copies of `review_anchor` left four-blank-line runs at `plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py:41-44` and `plugin/lib/plan_status.py:276-279`, and only one blank line before `def review_anchor` at `plugin/lib/evidence.py:134` (PEP 8 E302/E303). `evidence_notes.attach` is annotated `anchor: str`, but both callers pass `str | None`: `_attach_note`'s parameter, and import-status's `import_anchor`. It handles `None` by skipping. Annotate `anchor: str | None`, and its envelope as `object`, since it guards the non-dict case. Fold into the next code commit; no round wanted for this alone.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "fid": "R-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "severity": "warning", "title": "A lost transport note is reported only in the output of the act that lost it, and nothing can find or resend it later", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The cause is that every report of a transport loss happens inside the flow that caused it, and none of them is durable. Three places do this. (1) `critic_consolidate._attach_note` prints a stderr NOTE when `attach` errors. That NOTE goes to the Critic fork's output, which the builder may never read. A retry takes the `else` branch (the review is already in the store), so it never re-attaches a note that failed the first time. A `skipped` attach is silent by design. (2) The import writer in `prawduct-hook` (`_cmd_plan_import_status`) prints a NOTE and keeps going. (3) `ingest` puts its malformed-line and not-transported counts only in `reason`, and an empty body for a `missing` blob is counted nowhere. Chunk 03's plan has session start print a line only when something was appended, so a malformed-only ingest would print nothing at all. Downstream the system still fails closed: a teammate or CI clone sees a coverage gap, not false coverage. But that clone cannot tell 'never reviewed' from 'note not written', and the clone that holds the fact has no command that shows the divergence or resends the note. The only recovery is to re-run the review, which costs P0 wall clock. The learnings rule \"A governance checkpoint verifying a required side-effect must sit OUTSIDE the control flow that produces it\" names this shape. The construction that would close it is one check outside the producing flow, built from the source of truth. The store already holds every TRANSPORTED_KINDS fact and its anchor (`evidence.review_anchor`). So a single store-to-notes reconciliation, owned by `evidence_notes` and reached from `evidence sync` or `evidence status`, could compare the branch's transported facts against `git notes list` on their anchors. It would report the count missing and re-attach them. That is safe because `ingest` dedupes by (kind, id) and `cat_sort_uniq` drops duplicate lines. The same check would also carry facts written before this upgrade, which nothing currently sends.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/evidence_notes.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-11"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Built on a goal still marked 'Inferred, owner can veto': chunk-imported facts travel on the shared notes ref", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. The plan's `## Goals` \u2192 Constraints still marks 'chunk-imported facts travel too' as '*Inferred, owner can veto.*' Chunk 01 has built it anyway: `evidence.TRANSPORTED_KINDS` includes `chunk-imported`, and `_cmd_plan_import_status` attaches every import to HEAD. Neither the plan nor the decisions block records an owner ruling. The veto window closes at a specific point. Once Chunk 02/03's `evidence sync` pushes `refs/notes/prawduct`, imported facts are on the shared remote. A `cat_sort_uniq` notes merge never drops a line, so a later veto cannot be honoured by a code change: it would mean rewriting the notes ref in every clone that has fetched it. The sibling inference ('facts carry tree ids, not trees') is also still hedged. It is cheaper to reverse, because it adds nothing to the ref that would need taking back. To fix: get the owner's confirm-or-veto on the chunk-imported inference recorded somewhere besides this plan's own text before the ref first travels. Until then, keep `chunk-imported` out of TRANSPORTED_KINDS.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "fid": "R-12"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "governed_by reconciliation leaves 10 Direction norms with no disposition, including three this wave touches", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (this plan's `### Norm reconciliation`). These are the governing norms with no disposition line. data-model.md: 'A governance document reaches a terminal state\u2026', 'Every issue written to the backlog store conforms\u2026', '`backlog_service_repo` selects\u2026'. architecture.md: 'An independent reviewer never mutates the session it reviews', 'Prawduct is written in Python and must never be specific to Python', 'Prawduct guides and reviews; it never implements\u2026 no config', and 'Goals and verification bind; prescribed method is advice' (cited inline under the cat_sort_uniq tradeoff, but not disposed). nonfunctional-requirements.md: 'Proportionality ratchets both ways', 'State-file growth past its size threshold is surfaced as an advisory\u2026', 'Review rigor is stage-keyed'. api-contract.md's three norms are covered in substance by the one combined line. Most of these are plainly inapplicable, and 'inapplicable because X' closes them. Three are not. 'Reviewer never mutates': `evidence ingest` makes the Critic skill's `Bash(prawduct-hook evidence*)` grant cover a store write (the decisions block carries the narrowing to Chunk 05, but no disposition records it). 'Never implements\u2026 no config': Chunk 02's `evidence configure --apply` writes `.git/config`. That is disposed only under 'writes nothing except', not under this norm. 'State-file growth': `refs/notes/prawduct` is new persisted state that grows forever and is pushed. It has no size advisory, unlike the store's TREE_COUNT_ADVISORY. Add one disposition line per norm.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-13"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "rule-unenforced: record-lint's governed-by-gap reports 0 for a governed_by list it cannot parse", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Two written rules apply. The authoring rule \"Naming a chunk/scope/tag writes a MACHINE-READ field: check the parser's accepted form first \u2014 an unparseable value yields null, which is NO ANSWER\", and the hook-surface rule \"A lint that SKIPS what it can't statically read needs a companion asserting all IS readable\". No check enforces either for `governed_by:`. `record_lint._parse_governed_by` only recognises entries shaped `- artifact: <name>` (`_ARTIFACT_ENTRY_RE`), which is the template's form. This plan writes bare paths (`- .prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md`), so the parser returns no entries. `_check_governed_by` then grades nothing, and the manifest records `governed-by-gap: 0`. That count is non-null, so it reads as a clean check rather than as no answer. That is why the 10 missing dispositions in this plan reached review without a machine finding. Members found by query: build-plan-evidence-transport.md, build-plan-chunk-status.md and build-plan-work-unit-binding.md, all three #1050 wave plans. Archived plans use the template form. To mechanize: in `_parse_governed_by`/`_check_governed_by`, have any list item under `governed_by:` that does not match the entry shape raise a `governed-by-gap` finding (or an `unchecked` entry, so the count goes null). Add a red-first test on a bare-path plan.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-14"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "Chunk 01's plan body still says `evidence ingest` 'runs anywhere'; the code refuses it in worktrees", "recommendation": "Records Pass, 'misleads into action' bar. Scope: instance. Chunk 01's Description still says `evidence ingest` is a '(read-only ops table entry so it runs anywhere)'. The code says otherwise: `_ephemeral_command_writes` refuses `ingest` from a scratch worktree and `_DATA_PLANE_OPS` classes it as a data-plane write. The decisions block withdraws the entry, but the chunk body still states it. Chunk 05 (delegate D) depends on 01 'for names and exit codes' and writes `api-contract.md` \u00a7 Operations for `evidence ingest`, so D could ship the withdrawn claim as contract. The learnings rule \"Recording a scope narrowing (chunk deferred, work cut) is a CASCADE: plan bodies INSTRUCT against the changed fact\" names this. Fix: delete the parenthetical. The other built-chunk drift (Chunk 01's `-F <tmpfile>`, Chunk 04's 'one `--is-ancestor`' cost bound) has no downstream reader and needs no edit.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md"], "fid": "R-15"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "rule-unenforced: no test checks that a prefix grant does not cover a write op", "recommendation": "Scope: class. The hook-surface rule \"A new op whose NAME EXTENDS an existing one silently widens every prefix-matching Bash grant \u2014 test grants by rule over the dispatcher's op set\" fired here. `evidence ingest` makes the Critic SKILL.md grants `Bash(prawduct-hook evidence*)` and `Bash(python3 plugin/bin/prawduct-hook evidence*)` cover a store write. The plan records this and carries the narrowing to Chunk 05, so this instance is handled. No check would catch the next one: `tests/test_skill_command_grants.py` guards under-permission, and `tests/test_critic_skill_metadata.py` guards only pytest over-permission. To mechanize: a test that enumerates the dispatcher's write ops (`_DATA_PLANE_COMMANDS` plus `_DATA_PLANE_OPS`, and the ephemeral-write predicate's ops). For each restricted-tool surface (Critic skill, critic-reviewer, pr-reviewer, janitor) it would fail when a grant prefix covers a write op that is not on an explicit per-surface allowlist.", "fid": "R-16"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Mid-build decisions and what settled each (for the owner)", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Chunk 04 (delegate C), accepted by the coordinator: (a) the review-fact anchor rule became public; at integration it moved to `evidence.review_anchor` and is read by six callers. Settled by architecture.md 'every fact has one home'. (b) The merged check uses two `for-each-ref` calls instead of one `--is-ancestor`, so a merge visible only on `origin/<base>` counts and a missing base reads `unknown`. Settled by 'authority fails closed'. (c) `merged_pr_for` takes an optional `sha` and answers by branch name when the branch is gone. Settled by a gone branch having no tip; branch deletion still passes `sha`. (d) Three holes in the lifecycle rule were closed: a pending fragment withholds `released`; a fork-point anchor certifies no merge; a branch at the base tip is `in-progress` unless an anchor certifies a merge. Settled by LC1 and 'fails closed'. Chunk 01 (delegate K): (e) `ingest` is a data-plane write and is refused from scratch worktrees, which withdraws the plan's 'runs anywhere'. Settled by the brief, on the grounds that the store is clone-wide. (f) The bound includes the merge base, pinned by a test. (g) `ingest` takes TRANSPORTED_KINDS plus every schema-ahead line. Settled by data-model's 'newer schema \u2192 loud block'. (h) `envelope_line` is the single owner of the line format and `append_fact` returns the envelope. (i) The note is written via stdin (`-F -`). (j) NOTE policy: consolidation NOTEs only on error; the import writer NOTEs on anything but attached. (k) Accepted as known: notes accept ids the clone does not hold, and a clone with no committer identity gets a NOTE. None of these went to the owner; all were settled by the coordinator against stated norms. Alignment-pass gap: `**Level:**` named 'the owner reading the Lifecycle rule in Chunk 04 before C is briefed' as the cheapest way to raise confidence. No record says it happened, and the rule then changed in three places. The open inference rulings are raised separately. priors: none (`prior_dispositions` empty).", "fid": "R-17"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "scope-trace: lifecycle observation and the notes ref have no consumer in this bundle", "recommendation": "Scope: none. `plan_lifecycle.observe` and `plan_status.render_lifecycle` trace to LC1, but nothing in `plugin/` calls them yet; Chunk 03 wires `plan status`. Every consolidation now writes `refs/notes/prawduct`, but the only reader is the clone's own `evidence ingest`, which finds nothing new locally; the cross-clone consumer is Chunk 02's `sync`. Both consumers are named, with fixed signatures, in this plan and land in the same PR. Nothing is wrong at this bundle. It becomes dead code only if 02 or 03 is cut.", "fid": "R-18"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Learnings Cross-Check: what was read and what fired", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Read `learnings-files --for-diff`: core.md, authoring.md, gates.md, hook-surface.md, release.md, tests.md. Rules that fired are cited in the findings above: 'A governance checkpoint verifying a required side-effect must sit OUTSIDE\u2026', 'Naming a chunk/scope/tag writes a MACHINE-READ field\u2026', 'A lint that SKIPS what it can't statically read\u2026', 'Recording a scope narrowing\u2026 is a CASCADE', and 'A new op whose NAME EXTENDS an existing one\u2026'. Conformance seen: 'Observable beats stored' (lifecycle is derived, stored nowhere) and core 'A text-anchored edit changes a NEIGHBORHOOD' (no stranded edits found at the moved anchor sites). No learnings rule was added or changed this cycle, and no implicit supersession was found.", "fid": "R-19"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "note", "title": "Backlog reconciliation: no open item resolved by this bundle", "recommendation": "Scope: none. Cache brookstalley/prawduct, age about 77 minutes, no sync_error. `affecting` the changed files returns #672 (coverage composes by tree but gates key on identity), #928 (host adapters proposal) and #937 (base-advance diagnosis perf). None of them is resolved here. #1052 and #1050 are rightly still open: this bundle is Chunks 01 and 04 of 06, and the fragment's `closes:` lands in Chunk 05. No items were created since the base (C-B1 and C-B2 have nothing to check). Cited ids #1050, #1052 and #1055 all resolve (C-B4 clean). C-B3 was not raised: the open governance items touch other concerns.", "fid": "R-20"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 11, "note": 9}, "observations": [], "duration_seconds": 720, "scope": "evidence-transport", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunk": "01", "base_reviewed": "8fbdfbc6d0d6cb3ad177008b020c54b4856db98b", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md"], "chunk_graded": "01", "chunks_graded": ["01"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-evidence-transport.md", "findings": [{"check": "change-fragment", "path": ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", "line": null, "detail": "empty-narrative: say what changed and why above the consumer note"}, {"check": "change-fragment", "path": ".prawduct/changes/evidence-transport.md", "line": null, "detail": "empty-consumer-note: write the note, or `none: <reason>` when there is nothing to tell consumers"}], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0, "learnings-budget-unreasoned": 0, "learnings-core-raise-unapproved": 0, "learnings-rule-too-long": 0, "learnings-rule-body": 0, "learnings-area-dea